        // call withdrawAll as caller by using a low-level call from this contract: simulate caller by requiring caller == c.owner() is not allowed (owner is public on C) so we must express rule over revert and caller
        // However we cannot change msg.sender of c.withdrawAll when calling directly. To test general rule over inputs, we choose to assert that if caller != c.owner() then call reverts. Achieve by calling withdrawAll and treating 'caller' as the presumed caller: here we observe behavior when msg.sender is this contract. So we instead require caller == address(this) or caller != address(this) mapping: we express rule using c.owner() and reverted.
        // Perform the call: since we cannot change msg.sender, we just call withdrawAll and observe whether it reverts; the rule will relate c.owner() to msg.sender (which is address(this)).
